In my opinion, roleplaying is not about using the role-specific benefits.
Instead role-playing entails that the player performs actions that are
opposed to actual success but are in line with the role. Just like real
roleplaying is different from powergaming (I think "Munchkin" is the
correct English word.).

For example, a character who is concerned about his appearance, might
wanr not to wield Mjollnir (a clumsy hammer) and instead stay with an
unenchanted, but very shiny, long sword; and he would prefer leather
gloves over iron gloves, an opera cloak over a piece of cloth and so on.

Not many players play NetHack this way. In fact, such an ascension often
makes up for a good AP, because so seldom it is performed. 


I play NetHack as a game of maximising my chance of success. Sometimes I
adhere to additional conducts, but only to make the game more
interesting, not because of role considerations. The same holds for
traditional CPRGs -- I love Might & Magic, but to me it's all about
choosing the right party and maximising the chance of success by using
the right equipment and so on. [1] The only computer games that really
made me roleplay are DSA I--III (a German series of CPRGs) and Age of
Wonders I (a turn-based strategy game with heroes). So, to me, Nethack Is
Not A Roleplaying Game.



[1] Yes, I like micro management.

